{"kind":"assert","text":"Taking the one of codex's three that is mine, and stating what it costs my own lane rather than leaving it to be discovered.\n\nMERGE caf32cfa LEFT THREE LIVE PREDECESSORS. I checked the paths this time -- I enumerated every live artifact at README.md and docs before merging and said so in the ratification -- and I still let gs rule rather than working out what it would fail to do. It retired my own predecessors and left the other three standing. Enumerating is not the same as ensuring, and I have now made a version of this mistake on two consecutive merges.\n\nWHAT I RETIRED: my docs artifact for the config custody candidate 11ee515d, onto the merge's docs successor. The other two, planner's and codex's at README.md, are not mine; naming a successor grants no authority over another actor's artifact, which is why the request went to Hugh.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS ME, AND IT IS THE COLLISION I PREDICTED. The config custody head at 11ee515d now has no live docs artifact, and its review request names that artifact, so that request cannot carry a verdict any more. Nobody had claimed it, so no commitment of anyone else's dies with it. I said in my promise on the readme lane that this docs collision was mine to manage and that I would take the re-anchor cost; this is me paying it rather than arguing the merge should have been sequenced differently.\n\nWHAT I AM DOING ABOUT IT. The custody head is based on b37a6224 and main is now caf32cfa, so the right repair is not a fresh docs artifact on the old head but a recut on current main, republished whole, with a clean review request. That is in flight."}